That order is correct: get the four monthly values first, then choose the metric set. The series decides which metrics discriminate; picking metrics first invites choosing the ones that look stable.

When you pull the numbers, record for each month, for each candidate metric:
- the definition (what is counted, which queues, which clock);
- *n* (tickets, incidents, or both);
- the value (mean/p95 wait, error rate, SLA hit rate, backlog—only those you can define the same way every month);
- any failure class that the headline metric does not capture (same cause, same queue, or severity), as a count or rate, not as a narrative.

Without definition, *n*, and out-of-metric classes, a four-row table can still hide a material exception.

Do not collapse to before-and-after until you have seen the month-to-month spread. Do not draft the summary until the metrics and any one-line exception are fixed by those figures.

Unknown that still changes the metric set: whether Sandra’s decision is net improvement or whether the queues can be left unsupervised.

