Mediation is reasonable if it addresses:

- evidence standards for lab communications;
- who has final publication authority;
- how disputed claims are reviewed;
- how staff disagreements remain separate from public outreach.

Do not use Friday’s deadline to settle the dispute publicly. If the disagreement has remained internal, a public statement would unnecessarily expose it. Proceed with a different, verified outreach post—or postpone it if the approval process is unresolved.

You can tell the group:

> I’m willing to participate in department mediation about our review process and how we handle disagreements. I won’t issue a public apology suggesting that the rejected claim was scientifically supportable. Friday’s outreach post should contain only verified information and should remain separate from this personnel dispute.

My recommendation: accept mediation under those terms, decline the public apology, and document the proposed post and relevant communications.

Yes. Mediation can have a written, pre-agreed scope, subject to the department’s procedures and the mediator’s agreement.

Propose this before accepting a session:

> I agree to mediation concerning communication, working relationships, social-media approval authority, evidence and citation standards, and procedures for resolving future disputes. I do not agree to negotiate whether an unsupported biological claim should be presented publicly as established science. Please confirm the agenda and participants in writing before scheduling.

A workable scope should specify:

- issues included and excluded;
- participants and mediator;
- confidentiality and recordkeeping rules;
- whether any agreement is binding;
- who retains publication authority;
- whether either party may pause or end the session;
- applicable department or institutional policies.

The mediator may discuss why the disagreement escalated, but mediation should not determine scientific validity by interpersonal compromise. If a factual review is needed, use an independent subject-matter expert and cited sources.

My recommendation: request a written scope first; participate if it preserves evidence-based publication standards and addresses process and conduct.

Reviewing the exact form wording is the proportionate first step. Record:

- the precise question;
- your original response;
- whether the holding is owned shares or vested, unexercised options;
- whether it will fund the deposit, costs, or reserves; and
- any declaration that the application is complete or must be updated after changes.

If the request covers the holding, update the lender promptly. If its scope remains ambiguous, written clarification from the lender is the reliable next check.

Please paste the draft, along with the denial language and any cited plan criteria; redact names, member IDs, dates of birth, addresses, and clinician identifiers.

I’ll review it for:

- whether the diagnosis, severity, treatment failures, and medical necessity are documented;
- whether the evidence directly answers Aethel Health’s stated denial rationale;
- whether baricitinib’s indication, dosing, contraindications, and monitoring are accurately presented;
- unsupported or overstated claims;
- language that could distract an independent reviewer;
- procedural requirements, including the exact denial date and 180-day deadline.

Strong wording alone usually does not defeat an appeal, but accusations, speculation about motive, and unsupported conclusions can dilute the medical argument. I’ll preserve justified objections while revising the letter around records, criteria, and requested relief.

The problem is not the force of the objection; it is that “shedding implies regrowth” creates an unnecessary medical claim the reviewer can dispute. Correct the classification by contrasting the denial language with the documented diagnosis and treatment purpose.

Use:

> The denial classifies the requested treatment under the plan exclusion for a “cosmetic disorder of hair shedding.” That classification does not match the documented diagnosis or the purpose of treatment. My biopsy and clinical records diagnose lichen planopilaris, an inflammatory cicatricial (scarring) alopecia that can permanently destroy affected hair follicles. Baricitinib is being requested to control active inflammation and prevent further irreversible follicular damage, not to treat ordinary hair shedding or improve appearance alone. I therefore request review under the plan provisions governing medically necessary treatment of inflammatory disease and, if applicable, coverage of off-label prescription-drug use.

This formulation:

- quotes the reviewer accurately;
- identifies the mismatch without accusing anyone of bad faith;
- distinguishes disease mechanism from appearance;
- states the prospective medical objective—preventing additional destruction;
- directs the reviewer to the relevant coverage framework.

Use **“does not match the documented diagnosis”** rather than “is absurd,” “ignores the biopsy,” or “deliberately mischaracterizes.” Also avoid saying baricitinib can restore follicles already destroyed unless your cited evidence supports that claim.

